WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.850
- I am really feeling the bearded vibe

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.070
I love a beard on a man.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.210
- Beard is new for me.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.043
- It is?

00:00:06.043 --> 00:00:06.876
- Yeah new and beard.

00:00:06.876 --> 00:00:07.709
- Is it for work? Or for life?

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:09.510
- I grew it for work, but now it's

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.030
for life because it serves two purposes

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.663
I'm loosing a lot of the
elasticity in my jaw,

00:00:15.663 --> 00:00:16.820
(crowd laughs)

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.821
and also it's like having
a pet on your face.

00:00:18.821 --> 00:00:19.654
(crowd laughs)

00:00:19.654 --> 00:00:20.487
- (laughs) You just could pet it.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:21.870
- Never alone. I'm never alone.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.460
- What do your kids think of your beard?

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.388
- They're amused and
bemused by everything I do.

00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:27.221
- (laughs)

00:00:27.221 --> 00:00:28.450
- They just think I'm old.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.080
- That and mine too, it's
super fun when you work hard.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.959
(laughs) So you play a
cool dad in "Indebted",

00:00:33.959 --> 00:00:34.940
- Yeah very laid back.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.200
- But tell us about the show
for people that don't know.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.697
- The show is basically the line is,

00:00:39.697 --> 00:00:41.460
"baby boomers who go bust,"

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.260
and they gotta move in with their kids.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.887
And so Fran Drescher and I play

00:00:44.887 --> 00:00:47.815
amazingly good looking grandparents,

00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:48.790
- (laughs)

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:49.623
(crowd applauds)

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.456
Yes you do.

00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:52.027
- Don't applaud.

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:54.180
- (laughs)

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.710
- And through years of crazy spending

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.978
and bad investments we
move in with our kids.

00:01:00.978 --> 00:01:02.470
I'm having a great time cause

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.147
my character is like a little
bit of a Lebowski type.

00:01:05.147 --> 00:01:06.282
- I love that.

00:01:06.282 --> 00:01:08.274
- He likes his special chocolates and

00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:11.217
he's really crazy in love with her.

00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:12.050
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.990
- So he's relaxing, but the parents

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.420
have to parent the parents.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.520
- Yeah, that does happen in
life, but here's my question.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.413
They're rebooting everything,
why not reboot "Wings"?

00:01:23.413 --> 00:01:24.246
(crowd applauds)

00:01:24.246 --> 00:01:25.079
Cause I loved "Wings".

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:25.912
(crowd cheers)

00:01:25.912 --> 00:01:28.680
I mean literally they're
rebooting everything.

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:34.630
- I'd do it, most
everybody became gainfully

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.330
employed after "Wings", Tim Daly

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.319
is the handsomest man in North America.

00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:40.152
- (laughs)

00:01:40.152 --> 00:01:44.840
- Still. And Tony Shalhoub,
Amy Yasbeck, Crystal Bernard,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.200
David Schramm, Tom Haden Church.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.033
- It was such a great cast.

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:48.480
- It was a great cast, great show.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.313
- Great chemistry.

00:01:49.313 --> 00:01:51.720
- Great chemistry and we had so much fun,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.720
but I'm finally back on a great sitcom.

00:01:56.754 --> 00:01:58.220
- And you like the chemistry
of this one too ya?

00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.380
- They're great. Well I've known Fran

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.870
for a long time, so it's
fun to work with her

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:02.737
we're both from...
- I've known her since

00:02:02.737 --> 00:02:04.553
"The Nanny" (laughs)

00:02:04.553 --> 00:02:06.590
- And then I found out "The Nanny"

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.260
is a global event, okay. We went

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.820
to these TV kind of sales.
- Comic-con kind of thing?

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.299
- It was like a Sony thing right.

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:16.200
- Oh okay.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.560
- So there were TV affiliates from

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.590
all over the world, zoom they saw her,

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.490
people from Tokyo,
people from Middle East,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.273
and it was great.
- Mr. Sheffield

00:02:24.273 --> 00:02:25.700
Yes, yes. (crowd laughs)

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.533
- Yes.

00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:27.999
- I watched it religiously, yes.

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:29.249
- Apparently you did,
yeah. That's an uncanny...

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:30.400
- "Wings" as well I'm just saying,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.233
- Thanks, thanks.

00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:32.430
- This is crazy, you've been in more

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.140
than a hundred different shows and movies.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:34.973
- Wow.

00:02:34.973 --> 00:02:35.816
- That's crazy!

00:02:35.816 --> 00:02:36.649
(crowd applauds)

00:02:36.649 --> 00:02:38.509
- What a career!
- Yes!

00:02:38.509 --> 00:02:39.970
That's awesome! Do you remember

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.590
your first performance? It doesn't

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.090
have to be professional,
just first performance.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.230
- My first performance. So I was in

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.360
the third grade and we adapted

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.170
the book "Where the Wild Things Are,"

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.424
Maurice Sendak, Max goes to the island,

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:58.430
and I managed to land the supporting

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.589
role of the dragon, who can be seen on

00:03:00.589 --> 00:03:04.420
page three as Max goes,
- (laughs)

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.810
- and that was it. I wasn't
the lead, so I guess I

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.268
felt compelled to start
ad-libbing profusely.

00:03:12.268 --> 00:03:13.101
- You felt competitive.

00:03:13.101 --> 00:03:14.908
_ I felt competitive. I just felt

00:03:14.908 --> 00:03:18.250
not as intense as the connection

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.823
I feel with this audience.
This beautiful audience.

00:03:19.823 --> 00:03:22.686
(crowd cheers)
- Good answer! Good answer.

00:03:22.686 --> 00:03:24.706
(crowd applauds)

00:03:24.706 --> 00:03:27.560
- And I started ad-libbing as the dragon

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.140
and people started laughing, and

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.050
it was like a shot, and that was the end.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:31.883
Or the beginning!

00:03:31.883 --> 00:03:32.807
- Oh, you liked it, you were like

00:03:32.807 --> 00:03:34.140
"that's cool! Like making people laugh."

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.152
- That was it, and at some point when

00:03:36.152 --> 00:03:38.580
people didn't respond
to a particular scene

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.547
I said cut! You know, I was like seven.

00:03:40.547 --> 00:03:41.380
(crowd laughs)

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.213
And I was getting laughs.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.250
- That's amazing! To know that early.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.170
Wow. What a blessing.
Well one of your first

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.980
acting jobs was actually dubbing cartoons?

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.723
- Not dubbing cartoons,
but dubbing foreign films.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.100
When I was growing up, on TV they

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.220
would have these gladiator films,

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.753
or speed racer cartoons,
right speed racer.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.160
So I got a job dubbing
voices in foreign films,

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:11.184
and if you don't know what dubbing is,

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:14.720
and we do it in American
TV and movies too,

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.440
if the sound is bad you gotta go into

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.870
a studio and then redo the sentence.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.870
For instance, let's do it now.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:25.040
- Okay, let's do it. Oh I like
it, it's a game, I'm in, Yes!

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:27.433
That's my energy.
(crowd laughs)

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.187
- So excited. So contagious.

00:04:29.187 --> 00:04:30.428
- Yeah, sorry.

00:04:30.428 --> 00:04:33.094
(crowd laughs)

00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:35.437
- Now just say this line, say the line

00:04:35.437 --> 00:04:39.230
"Melvin, I have to de-vein
the shrimp," just say it.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.150
- Melvin I have to de-vein the shrimp.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.370
- Now I heard a sound, so we have to

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.990
take that out, you have to
- Move my mouth.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:48.680
- Yeah, but it's in another language,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.730
so I have to find the line to fit

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.625
the same kind of mouth flaps,

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:54.881
(crowd laughs)

00:04:54.881 --> 00:04:56.464
but I have to put in a line that's

00:04:56.464 --> 00:04:58.350
germane to whatever film I'm trying to...

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.900
- So like, if I'm gonna mouth that...

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.537
- Say Melvin, what did you have to say?

00:05:01.537 --> 00:05:03.320
- Melvin I have to de-vein the shrimp.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.900
- Okay, so now don't do any voice now.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:05.733
- Okay.

00:05:05.733 --> 00:05:08.557
- Beep, beep, beep, there's a monster in

00:05:08.557 --> 00:05:13.390
- (laughs)
(crowd laughs)

00:05:15.089 --> 00:05:15.922
- Listen.

00:05:17.798 --> 00:05:18.720
- (laughs) I wasn't ready for the...

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:19.988
- You're a terrible beeper.

00:05:19.988 --> 00:05:20.997
(crowd laughs)

00:05:20.997 --> 00:05:23.650
- I wasn't ready for the
voice change and all of it.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:24.650
I wasn't ready.
- I just have to approximate

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:27.040
your character, your whole...

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.850
- That's amazingly hard.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:29.740
- Let's do it again.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:30.852
- I can't look at you.

00:05:30.852 --> 00:05:31.685
(crowd laughs)

00:05:31.685 --> 00:05:32.750
- Don't look at me.
- Melvin I have to

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.148
de-vein the shrimp.
- If I had a nickel for

00:05:34.148 --> 00:05:35.065
every time I've heard that all right.

00:05:35.065 --> 00:05:35.898
- (laughs)

00:05:35.898 --> 00:05:36.850
- Now listen, I'm going
to count down ready.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.120
Beep, beep, beep, there's a monster

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.213
in the living room. So that's it.

00:05:43.213 --> 00:05:45.680
(crowd cheers)

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.190
- So randomly enough, I walked in and

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.770
watched my daughter, she's five,

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.028
and I let her just pick
something on Netflix,

00:05:53.028 --> 00:05:55.440
I was doing something, and all of a sudden

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.710
I'm like, why are they speaking Spanish?

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.410
She watches things that are from Spain,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:01.590
but they'll try and do them in English,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.700
like half the episode is English,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.060
they do that a lot still.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.960
- Yes, part of the problem is in different

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.550
languages there are different cadences,

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.180
and different rhythms, so that's why

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.620
if you watch a lot of old movies you'll

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.206
hear people talking,
like this, over there.

00:06:14.206 --> 00:06:15.660
Because they're trying to
- (laughs)

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.120
- fit in dialogue into
foreign mouth flapping.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.830
- Oh my god, that's hard! And

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.080
hard not to laugh, obviously!

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:24.913
- It's hard not to laugh?

00:06:24.913 --> 00:06:25.746
- Mhm.

00:06:25.746 --> 00:06:26.579
- You need practice.

00:06:26.579 --> 00:06:28.760
- I know, I know, you know
what, I should just steer clear.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:29.593
- Forget it.

00:06:29.593 --> 00:06:30.426
- I feel like I should forget it.

00:06:30.426 --> 00:06:31.265
- You're doing too much already.

00:06:31.265 --> 00:06:34.530
- (laughs) So you developed another

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:35.850
vocal skill, what is it?

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.680
- I can do a cricket.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.920
- A cricket? What were you in

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.580
Pinocchio or something?
(crowd laughs)

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.140
How did you develop a cricket sound?

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.470
- Well because there was a guy on "Wings,"

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:48.120
one of the techs, one of the grips,

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.580
named Mundo, and he used to very kind of

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.297
secretly throw in a cricket sound,

00:06:54.297 --> 00:06:56.572
and it would drive the
sound people insane.

00:06:56.572 --> 00:06:59.110
- (gasps) I love him.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:00.486
- So I have to just wet my whistle.

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:02.738
- (mumbles)

00:07:02.738 --> 00:07:06.270
- (cricket sound)

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.868
- That is impressive!

00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:09.356
- Is it?
(crowd cheers)

00:07:09.356 --> 00:07:11.325
(crowd applauds)

00:07:11.325 --> 00:07:12.225
- That's impressive!

00:07:12.225 --> 00:07:13.745
- (laughs) You need to calm down.

